# TypeScript Standards

## Type Safety
- Never use `any` type - use specific interfaces or union types
- Provide default values instead of optional parameters with `?`
- Use interfaces for object structures, prefix with `I`
- Use types for unions and primitives, prefix with `T`

## Interface Design
```typescript
// Correct interface structure
interface IUserPayload {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  role: TUserRole;
  // Provide defaults instead of optional
  createdAt: string = new Date().toISOString();
  updatedAt: string = new Date().toISOString();
}

type TUserRole = 'Admin' | 'User' | 'Guest';
```

## Async/Await Standards
- Always use try/catch blocks with async/await
- Use `console.error()` in catch blocks for error logging
- Provide default values when possible to handle errors gracefully
- Don't re-throw errors if providing defaults

```typescript
async function fetchUserData(): Promise<IUser[]> {
  try {
    const response = await this.apiService.getUsers();
    return response.data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching users:', error);
    return []; // Graceful default
  }
}
```

## Guard Clauses
- Use early returns for error conditions
- Handle flag-based conditions with guard clauses
- Reduce nesting with early exits

## Immutable Patterns
- Use spread operator for arrays: `[...existingArray, newItem]`
- Use object spread for updates: `{...existingObject, updatedField: newValue}`
- Consider performance implications for large data structures